A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their containing template. Source directives attach to the next eligible declaration or dependency clause.

### Two locations

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]

    $export
    greeting #= "Hello"
]

`$export` makes a declaration public in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth`. It can also mark a direct-selection dependency clause as a public re-export.

`$config` marks one explicitly typed compile-time `#` binding in `config.moth` as a build input:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

The name is the input key, the type is its contract and the initializer is the fallback. Only `$config` bindings may omit their initializer.

Source directives occupy their own line. Comment-only lines may intervene, but a blank line breaks attachment. Each item accepts at most one source modifier.

### Operands

Some template directives accept `$name value` or `$name key = value`. Parenthesized forms such as `$name(...)` are invalid. `$export` and `$config` take no operands.

Template-head directives are `$before`, `$after`, `$fresh`, `$slot`, `$in`, `$note`, `$todo`, `$doc`, `$raw`, `$md`, `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code`.

Entries are selected by the `@entry.moth` filename, not a directive. See @../directives (Directives), @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API) and @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build inputs).
